(THE DRIFT) Tj
/F1 10 Tf
T*
T*
(Released creatures do not simply vanish. They drift into other players' worlds) Tj
T*
(with their lineage attached, so you can meet someone else's bloodline in your) Tj
T*
(own grass. Breeding, releasing, trading and exploring become one loop.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf
(CO-OP) Tj
/F1 10 Tf
T*
T*
(Two players can share a battle. That is rare in this genre, and it is a) Tj
T*
(deliberate differentiator. Multiplayer is always an enhancement and never a) Tj
T*
(dependency: if the connection is down, the game still starts, plays and saves) Tj
T*
(on its own.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf
(THE LOOK) Tj
/F1 10 Tf
T*
T*
(The target is HD-2D - hand-drawn pixel creatures standing in a three-dimensional) Tj
T*
(world, with a shallow depth of field that makes the scene look like a small) Tj
T*
(diorama, plus real lighting, glow and drifting particles. It is exactly what) Tj
T*
('a Game Boy game, remade' means today.) Tj
T*
T*
(It is also a rendering layer rather than a different game: the world stays a) Tj
T*
(grid of tiles underneath, so the ambitious part cannot break the part that) Tj
T*
(works. It is planned as a deliberate late stage, with a graphics setting that) Tj
T*
(falls back to flat, crisp pixel art if a device struggles.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf
(HOW IT IS STRUCTURED) Tj
/F1 10 Tf
T*
T*
(Chapter One is a complete game of roughly ten to twelve hours - four badge) Tj
T*
(challenges, about twenty areas, thirty-six creatures - with its own beginning,) Tj
T*
(middle and ending, and threads left open on purpose. Later chapters extend the) Tj
T*
(story, the region and the systems. Every chapter ends with something finished,) Tj
T*
(so stopping is never a loss.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf
(TWO VERSIONS) Tj
/F1 10 Tf
T*
T*
(The game ships as Dawn and Dusk. Most creatures appear in both; each version has) Tj
T*
(a handful of exclusives. That gives trading a real purpose, and gives the) Tj
T*
(multiplayer room a reason to exist beyond battling.) Tj
ET
